\date{2026-07-11T00:13:50Z}
\import{base-macros}
\author{kellenkanarios}
\title{A reconvergence stack sequences a warp's divergent paths and can deadlock}
\tag{public}

\p{When a [warp](X9VZ) [diverges](OGCSM), the hardware needs to remember where the split paths rejoin. The classical mechanism is a \em{reconvergence stack}: each entry holds a next-PC, a reconvergence PC (the post-conditional join point), and an active mask. On a divergent branch the hardware pushes both sides and executes them one at a time, popping back to the reconvergence point as each side completes; entries are pushed largest-mask-first to bound stack depth.}

\p{The stack has a fatal flaw. A thread that exits a loop diverges and is stacked \em{below} threads still spinning inside it — so if those spinning threads wait on a lock the exited thread already holds, neither can proceed and the program deadlocks. This is why modern NVIDIA hardware abandons the stack for a barrier-based scheme: each divergence point is tracked by a 32-bit participation mask, letting the scheduler switch between branches arbitrarily and guarantee \em{independent thread progress}.}
